# IND_NAIS_2018 -- NITI Aayog National Strategy for Artificial Intelligence (2018). Codebook v2.0.1 first pass.
# Document class C: a discussion paper. Its own text states that "specifics (e.g. execution and
# financial implications) have been consciously avoided", so most units carry binding_status "proposed".
# unit_seq|T1,T2,T3,T4,T5,T6|binding_status|confidence|flag|flag_reason|justification
1|0,0,0,0,2,1|directed|High||None|"Hon'ble Finance Minister, in his budget speech for 2018-2019, mandated NITI Aayog to establish the National Program on AI", and "NITI Aayog has partnered with several leading AI technology players to implement AI projects in critical areas such as agriculture and health" -- an instructed programme with projects already "under various stages of implementation" (T5=2, directed). "The potential to position itself among leaders on the global AI map" and the aspiration to "replicate these solutions in other similarly placed developing countries" is competitiveness and technology-diplomacy framing without an instrument (T6=1).
2|0,0,0,1,2,1|proposed|High||None|"Sustained government intervention to tackle the existing challenges and constraints is needed" and the government is to "play a leading role in developing the implementation roadmap for AI" in sectors with the greatest externalities -- programmatic direction stated as a recommendation rather than a funded commitment (T5=2, proposed). "Responsible AI" appears in a list of dimensions with no framework, body or obligation attached (T4=1). "Scalable solutions for emerging economies" and tackling "global challenges from AI's perspective" is international framing (T6=1).
3|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|The unit is explicit that this is a pre-legislative discussion document: "while this paper includes several recommendations, some of which may be deemed disruptive, specifics (e.g. execution and financial implications) have been consciously avoided, since wider consultations and consensus building is needed". Building "the foundational R&D capability aimed at ensuring competitiveness in the long run" is therefore stated without programme or budget (T5=1). "Solving for India... can easily be extended to the rest of the emerging and developing economies" and India as "the ideal test-bed" is export and development-cooperation framing (T6=1).
4|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"NITI Aayog has decided to focus on five sectors that are envisioned to benefit the most from AI in solving societal needs" -- Healthcare, Agriculture, Education, Smart Cities and Infrastructure, and Smart Mobility and Transportation -- a defined sectoral programme with named priorities but no budget or implementing mechanism in this unit (T5=2). "A 'Garage' for emerging and developing economies" and "define its own brand of AI leadership" is international positioning (T6=1). At 343 words the unit sits inside the ceiling.
5|0,0,0,1,2,0|proposed|High||None|"The paper proposes a two-tiered structure to address India's AI research aspirations": a "Centre of Research Excellence (CORE)" and "International Centers of Transformational AI (ICTAI)" with private sector collaboration -- named proposed research institutions with defined mandates (T5=2, proposed). Among the listed barriers is "privacy and security, including a lack of formal regulations around anonymisation of data", which diagnoses a regulatory gap without proposing an instrument in this unit (T4=1). No content, surveillance or cross-border mechanism appears.
6|0,0,0,0,2,2|proposed|High||None|An umbrella research organisation, "moonshot research projects", "common computing and other related infrastructure for AI", reskilling mechanisms and a "National AI Marketplace (NAIM)" are proposed institutions and infrastructure with defined functions (T5=2). "Development of a dedicated supranational agency to channel research in solving big, audacious problems of AI -- 'CERN for AI'", together with "encouraging international collaboration", proposes a named cross-border research institution, which is the T6=2 anchor for a formalised international research structure. Both remain proposals in a discussion paper, so binding_status is proposed.
7|0,0,0,2,2,1|proposed|High||None|"A consortium of Ethics Councils at each Centre of Research Excellence can be set up and it would be expected that all COREs adhere to standard practice", and "the paper suggests establishing data protection frameworks and sectorial regulatory frameworks, and promotion of adoption of international standards", against named risks including "data usage without consent, risk of identification of individuals through data, data selection bias and the resulting discrimination of AI models" -- proposed institutional governance with defined content but no enumerated obligations or penalties, which is the T4=2 anchor. IP facilitation centres and training for "IP granting authorities, judiciary and tribunals" are proposed institutional capacity (T5=2). Adoption of international standards is soft alignment (T6=1). At 347 words the unit sits inside the ceiling.
8|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|The unit closes the executive summary with a comparative note -- "Governments in USA, UK, France, Japan and China have released their policy and strategy papers relating to AI" -- and then opens a technical primer describing AI capabilities and investment forecasts (T5=1: market context and a call to release a strategy paper, with no programme or budget). "In order to establish a leadership role, it is important for India to take the plunge" is competitiveness framing (T6=1). At 418 words the unit exceeds the 350-word ceiling and is flagged; it also spans a section boundary between the executive summary and the primer.
9|0,0,0,0,1,0|aspirational|Moderate||None|A technical primer on why the current wave of AI differs from earlier "AI winters", citing cloud market growth, falling storage costs and the growth of the global data sphere. The only thematic content is economic context -- the scale of the public cloud services market and of data generation -- with no Indian programme, instrument or commitment (T5=1). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is explanatory rather than policy text.
10|0,0,0,0,1,0|aspirational|Moderate||None|A continuation of the technical primer: GPUs versus CPUs, Google's Tensor Processing Unit, the definitions of machine learning and deep learning, and a cross-reference to Appendix I. The compute and open-source discussion is economic and infrastructural context only, with no Indian instrument, budget or commitment (T5=1). No obligation, right, content or cross-border mechanism appears.
11|0,0,0,0,0,0|n/a|High||None|A definitional taxonomy: weak versus strong AI, narrow versus general AI, and superintelligence, illustrated with chatbots and Deep Blue. The unit contains no policy content of any kind -- no mechanism, no actor under obligation, no programme, and no normative claim about how AI should be governed. All six themes are therefore 0 and binding_status is n/a.
12|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|The unit benchmarks other jurisdictions rather than committing India to anything: the US, France, Japan, China and the UK strategy releases, and the observation that "creation of 'data trusts', rolling out of digital connectivity infrastructure such as 5G / full fiber networks, common supercomputing facilities, fiscal incentives and creation of open source software libraries are some of the focus areas of various governments" (T5=1 -- other countries' instruments described, none adopted here). Comparative GDP claims, publication-volume league tables and the framing of an AI "race" is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
13|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|The unit continues the international benchmark: other governments' PhD and teacher-training targets, "dedicated public offices such as Ministry of AI (UAE), and Office of AI and AI Council (U.K.)", increased public R&D spend, industrial funds, and public-private "national teams". None of these is adopted by India here; the unit closes only with the conclusion that "the need of the hour is to develop a policy framework that will help set up a vibrant AI ecosystem in India" (T5=1). The warning that countries which "decide to wait for a few years" will not "match up to the current momentum" is competitiveness framing (T6=1). At 350 words the unit is exactly at the ceiling.
14|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|The framework is set out as three components -- economic opportunity, "AI for Greater Good", and "AI Garage for 40% of the world" -- and the economic case rests on a consultancy estimate that AI could "boost India's annual growth rate by 1.3 percentage points by 2035" (T5=1: economic vision with no instrument, budget or implementing body). Positioning India as "solution provider of choice for the emerging and developing economies (ex-China) across the globe" is export and technology-diplomacy framing without any control, agreement or funding mechanism (T6=1).
15|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Increased access to quality health facilities... inclusive financial growth... providing real-time advisory to farmers... building smart and efficient cities" are stated as opportunity areas rather than as programmes (T5=1). "India provides a perfect 'playground' for enterprises and institutions globally to develop scalable solutions which can be easily implemented in the rest of the developing and emerging economies", illustrated by a tuberculosis diagnostic that "could easily be rolled out to countries in South East Asia or Africa", is export framing (T6=1). No obligation, instrument or consequence appears.
16|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Solved in India (or more accurately, solved by Indian IT companies) could be the model going forward for Artificial Intelligence as a Service (AIaaS)" and the claim of a "proven track record in technology solution provider of choice" is service-export positioning with no instrument (T6=1). The reference to AI "capable of imparting quality education to India's linguistically diverse population" and to India's IT competence is economic framing (T5=1). Nothing is committed, funded or required.
17|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A catalogue of potential sectoral use cases -- "AI driven diagnostics, personalised treatment, early identification of potential pandemics" in healthcare; crop yield, pest detection and price prediction in agriculture; autonomous fleets and traffic management in mobility; personalisation and inventory management in retail (T5=1: illustrative use cases with no programme, budget or implementing body). Pandemic identification is epidemiological rather than individual monitoring, and the mobility and retail cases observe vehicles, engines and inventory, so Theme 1 is not engaged.
18|1,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Further sectoral use cases in manufacturing, energy, smart cities and education, presented as potential rather than as commitments (T5=1). Among them, "enhanced security through improved crowd management" in smart cities is crowd analytics, an explicit Theme 1 indicator whose object is people, but it appears as a one-line potential use case with no programme, budget or implementing body -- the T1=1 anchor. The education case, "predicting the need for student intervention to reduce dropouts or recommend vocational training", scores individuals for their own benefit and so falls to Theme 5 under the purpose test; energy and manufacturing observe grids and plant.
19|0,0,0,1,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|An analysis of what has driven sectoral AI adoption, concluding that "compelling business use cases... have been the biggest driver". "Regulatory barriers, privacy considerations, ethical issues, preference for human relationship have all played their roles in determining the readiness of a sector" names privacy and ethics as adoption factors without proposing any instrument, right or safeguard (T4=1); the adoption and investment analysis is economic context (T5=1). Confidence is Moderate because the unit reports survey findings rather than policy.
20|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A description of adoption in banking and manufacturing, including "development of credit scores through analysis of bank history or social media data; and fraud analytics for proactive monitoring and prevention of various instances of fraud, money laundering, malpractice, and the prediction of potential risks". These profile and score identified individuals, but the actors are banks and firms operating commercially; Theme 1 covers state monitoring of persons, so it is not engaged, and the practice is recorded here as market description rather than as proposed policy (T5=1). India being "ranked 3rd in the world in AI implementation in manufacturing, ahead of nations such as Germany" is comparative framing (T6=1).
21|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"NITI Aayog has evaluated various sectors that will be impacted by AI and has taken a conscious decision to focus on a select set of sectors where only private sector led initiatives will not lead to achieving desired societal outcomes" -- a decided sectoral prioritisation with a stated selection rule, which is programmatic direction rather than vision (T5=2), though still a recommendation in a discussion paper. The argument is that "externalities from adoption of AI far outweigh the economic returns realised by private initiatives, and hence the role of government becomes pivotal". No other theme is engaged.
22|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|The unit completes the focus-sector list and introduces the "AI + X" paradigm -- "take an existing process, and add AI" -- as an analytical frame for identifying incremental value, explicitly noting that AI today "should thus be regarded as an enhancement, or enabler of increased efficiency in previously existing processes" (T5=1: a conceptual framework with no programme or instrument). Smart Mobility is motivated by congestion, pollution and road accidents, which observes traffic rather than persons.
23|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A diagnostic profile of Indian healthcare: market size "expected to grow to USD280 billion by 2020", shortages of "0.76 doctors and 2.09 nurses per 1,000 population" against WHO benchmarks, non-uniform access, and the Tata Memorial Hospital catchment illustration (T5=1: sectoral economic and capacity analysis with no instrument, programme or commitment). No governance, content, surveillance or cross-border mechanism appears in the unit.
24|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"The Government of India has been making a series of large scale interventions to address India's healthcare challenges, viz. transformation of 1.5 lakh Health and Wellness Centers, developing district hospitals to cater to long-term care for non-communicable diseases, Ayushman Bharat Mission, promoting e-Health" -- named, operating government programmes at national scale (T5=2, enacted). The remainder documents affordability and out-of-pocket expenditure. Universal Health Coverage is described as a policy commitment rather than as an enforceable right, so Theme 4 is not engaged.
25|1,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|Moderate||None|"Through the Ayushman Bharat programme announced in Union Budget 2018... the Government of India has embarked on a path breaking journey", providing "insurance cover of INR 5 lakh per family per year" to "more than 10 crore families", alongside "a commitment of ~INR1,200 crore for Health and Wellness Centres" transforming 1.5 lakh Health Sub Centres from 2018 to 2022 (T5=2, enacted -- budgeted programmes at national scale). Beneficiaries are identified "based on the SECC database", a state population database governing access to a major benefit: that is data aggregation about identified individuals used for eligibility determination, which registers on Theme 1 at the level of a named capability with no monitoring or control purpose stated (T1=1). Confidence is Moderate because eligibility conferral sits at the edge of the Theme 1 purpose test. At 389 words the unit exceeds the ceiling and is flagged.
26|1,0,0,0,2,0|directed|Moderate||None|"A key component of HWCs will be universal screening for NCDs", and the government aims to establish "the National eHealth Authority (NeHA) which will strategise eHealth adoption, define standards and a framework for the health sector", "the Integrated Health Information Program (IHIP) to provide EHR to all citizens of India", and Electronic Health Record Standards -- named institutions and a national health data architecture (T5=2). An electronic health record "for all citizens of India" is population-scale aggregation of individual data by the state; its purpose is care delivery, which the purpose test routes to Theme 5, but the capability itself is named and universal, so Theme 1 registers at 1. Confidence is Moderate, and it is notable that the unit describes an all-citizen EHR with no accompanying privacy safeguard, which is an absence rather than a Theme 4 score.
27|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Market and opportunity description for AI in Indian healthcare: the global AI healthcare market "expected to register an explosive CAGR of 40% through 2021", and the claim that "AI solutions can augment the scarce personnel and lab facilities" and "help overcome the barriers to access" (T5=1: opportunity framing with no programme, budget or implementing body committed in this unit). The applications described are diagnostic and treatment support for the patients concerned, so the Theme 1 purpose test is not engaged, and no governance, content or cross-border mechanism appears.
28|0,0,0,0,2,0|directed|High||None|"NITI Aayog is in an advanced stage for launching a programme to develop a national repository of annotated and curated pathology images", entailing "a move towards 'Digital Pathology', which entails all glass slides generated being scanned at high resolution and magnification, followed by accurate, precise and comprehensive annotation" -- a named programme with specified components at the point of launch (T5=2, directed). The repository holds clinical images used to support diagnosis for the patients concerned, so the purpose test places it in Theme 5; the unit describes no consent, governance or privacy mechanism for the repository, which is an absence rather than a Theme 4 score.
29|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"NITI Aayog is working with Microsoft and Forus Health to roll out a technology for early detection of diabetic retinopathy as a pilot project", integrating "Microsoft's retinal imaging APIs" into the portable 3Nethra device so it works "in remote areas with nil or intermittent connectivity to the cloud" -- an operating pilot with named partners and a defined deployment setting (T5=2, enacted). Retinal screening identifies disease in the person screened for that person's care, so the Theme 1 purpose test is not met.
30|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"The Government of India has recently prioritised Doubling Farmers' Income as a National Agenda; putting considerable focus on supply chain perspectives in agriculture and market development in addition to productivity augmentation" -- a named national programme already in force (T5=2, enacted). The remainder analyses the sector's dependence on unpredictable variables, land degradation and pest resistance. The objects of concern are soil, water, crops and markets, so the Human Target Constraint keeps Theme 1 at 0.
31|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A diagnosis of agrarian distress: fluctuating growth, "globalised value chains leading to variability in commodity prices", monsoon variability, and "structurally inefficient domestic agricultural markets", with the observation that "there is no functional mechanism to track the capacity of storage facilities available to the farmer" (T5=1: sectoral analysis identifying gaps, with no instrument, programme or commitment). The tracking gap concerns storage capacity, which is physical infrastructure.
32|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Market sizing for AI in agriculture -- "USD432 million in 2016... expected to grow at the rate of 22.5% CAGR to be valued at USD2.6 billion by 2025" -- alongside smartphone and internet penetration figures and the claim that "digital farming and connected farm services can impact 70 million Indian farmers in 2020, adding USD9 billion to farmer incomes" (T5=1: market context with no Indian instrument). "Globally, digital and AI technologies are helping solve pressing issues across the agriculture value chain" is comparative framing (T6=1).
33|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A description of private AgTech activity: "approximately 50 Indian agricultural, technology based startups ('AgTech') raised USD313 million", with named examples using image recognition, drones and satellite imagery for yield prediction and soil health (T5=1: market description, not a state programme). "Image recognition and deep learning models have enabled distributed soil health monitoring without the need of laboratory testing infrastructure" observes soil and foliage -- things -- so Theme 1 is not engaged.
34|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Potential use cases in crop health monitoring: "AI can be used to predict advisories for sowing, pest control, input control", with agronomic factors "monitored up to the farm level through remote sensing", and image classification for weed removal, disease identification and harvesting (T5=1: illustrative use cases with no programme or commitment). The monitoring described is of crops, soil moisture and farm machinery, which the Theme 1 negative boundary routes explicitly to Theme 5.
35|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"Microsoft in collaboration with ICRISAT, developed an AI Sowing App... The app sends sowing advisories to participating farmers on the optimal date to sow", and "in 2017, the program was expanded to touch more than 3,000 farmers across the states of Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka", with measured yield increases "ranged from 10% to 30% across crops" -- an operating deployment at documented scale with measured outcomes (T5=2, enacted). Advisories are sent to farmers about their own fields; the data observed is weather, soil and crop phenology.
36|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"NITI Aayog and IBM have partnered to develop a crop yield prediction model using AI to provide real time advisory to farmers", drawing on "data from remote sensing (ISRO), soil health cards, IMD's weather prediction and soil moisture/temperature, crop phenology", and "the project is being implemented in 10 Aspirational Districts across the States of Assam, Bihar, Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh" -- an operating multi-state deployment with named partners and data sources (T5=2, enacted). The data fused is environmental and agronomic, not personal.
37|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A diagnosis of Indian school education: "retention rate of 70.7% at elementary level indicates that one-third of enrolled children drop out before completing Class 8", National Achievement Survey results showing that "over 60% of Class 5 students scored below 50% across subjects", and structural constraints such as multi-grade classrooms (T5=1: sectoral analysis with no instrument or programme committed). The National Achievement Survey is an assessment of learning outcomes at system level rather than a monitoring programme directed at identified individuals.
38|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Continued diagnosis: children "at risk of dropout due to various factors, such as inadequate school infrastructure, poor teachers, poor school readiness, language barriers", teacher vacancies "due to uneven distribution across different geographical areas", and generic teacher training that "end[s] up as wasted public expenditure" (T5=1: problem statement with no instrument). Dropout risk is discussed as a category of children to be helped, not as a scoring system to be built, so Theme 1 is not engaged in this unit.
39|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|Survey evidence on technology adoption in schools: "83% of the teachers surveyed use computers", but "a meagre 41% and 27% use technology for tracking student data and participating in forums respectively", and trained teachers are markedly more likely to use available technology (T5=1: adoption diagnostics with no programme or commitment). Confidence is Moderate because the unit reports third-party survey findings rather than policy. Student-data tracking is reported as an existing low-uptake teacher practice, not as a state monitoring programme.
40|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|EdTech market sizing -- India's digital learning market "projected to grow at a CAGR of 30%, reaching USD5.7 billion in 2020" -- and venture funding examples (T5=1: market description). The one forward-looking claim is a precondition rather than a commitment: "implementation of AI must be preceded by efforts to digitise records of teacher performance, student performance, and curriculum". That digitisation would aggregate individual records, but it is stated as a data-readiness prerequisite for educational delivery rather than for security, enforcement or control, so the Theme 1 purpose test is not met.
41|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Illustrative education use cases: intelligent tutoring "adapted to the child's proficiency level, learning style, and pace of learning", GradeGuardian's "Advisor Console that shows students at risk", and Content Technologies' customised material generation (T5=1: third-party products described as adaptable to India, with no programme, budget or commitment). Assessment of "student's level of attention or comprehension to appropriately design remedial instruction" profiles the learner in order to teach that learner, which the Theme 1 purpose test places in Theme 5.
42|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|Moderate|YES|Polysemic Uncertainty|"The AP government... has tied up with Microsoft" so that "based on specific parameters, such as gender, socio-economic demographics, academic performance, school infrastructure and teacher skills, an application powered by Azure Machine Learning processes the data pertaining to all students to find predictive patterns", and it "has identified about 19,500 probable dropouts from government schools in Visakhapatnam district" -- an operating state deployment at documented scale (T5=2, enacted). This is the case the Theme 1 purpose test (codebook 2.0.1) was written for: the state scores every student on protected characteristics, but the stated consequence is that "district education officials can intervene and help students who are most likely to drop out", a benefit to the person scored, so it falls to Theme 5. Flagged manually for adjudication because the profiling is population-scale and uses gender and socio-economic status, with no safeguard described.
43|1,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|"AI tools can be used to develop automated teacher posting and transfer systems, using analytics based on demand-supply gaps across schools in the State, candidate's prior postings, candidate preferences", and adaptive tools could generate professional development content "based on their performance, identification of their knowledge and skill gaps" (T5=1: recommended mechanisms with no programme, budget or implementing body). Automated posting and transfer produces a decision about where an identified public employee must work, which is administrative allocation with a consequence for the individual rather than a benefit conferred on them, so Theme 1 registers at the T1=1 anchor. Confidence is Moderate because personnel administration sits at the edge of the Theme 1 purpose test.
44|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"The Government of India has embarked on an ambitious initiative to set up Smart Cities across India... As part of the Smart Cities Mission, 99 cities have been selected with expected investment of INR2.04 lakh crores" -- a named, funded, operating national programme with defined strategic components (T5=2, enacted). "High crime rates" appears in the diagnosis of unplanned urbanisation but no surveillance or policing mechanism is described in this unit.
45|2,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|Moderate|YES|Polysemic Uncertainty|The unit contains both kinds of monitoring and is dual-coded. Smart electricity and water meters, leakage detection and waste management observe utilities and distribution networks -- things -- within operating smart-city programmes (T5=2, enacted). "Improving public safety: Cities in India today are hotbeds for a range of crimes. Smart cities aim to address the issues of increase in crime and increased risk of urban emergencies through improved city design and surveillance analytics", evidenced by Surat's network of "more than 600 surveillance cameras which will be expanded to all major locations in the city", observes people (T1=2). Under codebook v1 the smart-municipal-grid boundary would have routed the camera network to Theme 5; the v2 boundary clarification reverses that. Flagged on the mandatory T1 trigger.
46|1,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Smart-city use cases stated as possibilities: "use of AI to monitor patronage" of parks and public facilities to control lighting and maintenance, smart homes, and "predictive service delivery on the basis of citizen data" (T5=1: illustrative applications with no programme or commitment). Monitoring patronage of public spaces and predicting service needs from citizen data observes people, but no programme, budget, implementing body or enforcement consequence is described, which is the T1=1 anchor. Cybersecurity risk to city-scale infrastructure protects system integrity and belongs to Theme 5.
47|2,0,1,0,1,0|enacted|High|YES|Polysemic Uncertainty|"AI technology could provide safety through smart command centres with sophisticated surveillance systems that could keep checks on people's movement, potential crime incidents, and general security of the residents", and "in the city of Surat, the crime rate has declined by 27% after the implementation of AI powered safety systems" -- the outcome claim establishes that the system is operating, not proposed, which is the T1=2 anchor (binding_status enacted). "Social media intelligence platforms can provide aid to public safety by gathering information from social media and predicting potential activities that could disrupt public peace" is state monitoring of the public information space without any removal, labelling or platform obligation (T3=1). Cyber-attack detection protects infrastructure integrity and is Theme 5 baseline (T5=1). Flagged on the mandatory T1 trigger.
48|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Sectoral context for mobility: roads and railways carrying "almost 87% of total freight traffic" and "almost 90% of total traffic", the sector's emissions intensity, and the need for "efficient and safe transportation of both people and goods" (T5=1: sectoral analysis with no instrument, programme or commitment). The objects discussed are modal shares, freight and emissions.
49|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Road safety statistics: "the total number of road accidents in the country during 2015 was 501,423 which resulted to 146,133 fatalities", the disproportionate share of National Highways, and the lagging development of public transport infrastructure (T5=1: sectoral diagnosis with no instrument). Accident and modal-share data are aggregate transport statistics, not monitoring of identified individuals.
50|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Investing in the suite of autonomous vehicle technologies and exporting such vehicles represents a significant economic opportunity for India", and "it would be wise for Indian manufactures to invest in research and development of the broader suite of technologies that are essential for assistive AI" (T5=1: an investment recommendation addressed to manufacturers, with no state programme or funding). The export opportunity is competitiveness framing with no control or agreement mechanism (T6=1). The systems described assist drivers and hand control back on driver input.
51|0,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|High||None|"The recent initiative of the Government of India for announcing development of 100 Smart Cities is aimed at addressing this anomaly and catalyse smart strategies for urban planning which promote sustainable land use design and multimodal integration" -- a named national programme already announced (T5=2, enacted), with AI applied to "scheduling public transportation systems", accessibility and "real-time travel mode advisory based on predicted traffic situation". The objects optimised are schedules, traffic flow and greenfield road geometry.
52|2,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|Moderate|YES|Polysemic Uncertainty|"Through the use of an intelligent traffic management system including sensors, CCTV cameras, automatic number plate recognition cameras, speed detection cameras... and stop line violation detection systems and the use of AI, real time dynamic decisions on traffic flows such as lane monitoring, access to exits, toll pricing... enforcing traffic regulations through smart ticketing etc. can be made", with accident heat maps built from "driver behaviour at specific locations". Number-plate recognition, speed and violation detection identify vehicles and their drivers and issue penalties automatically: the purpose is enforcement, which the Theme 1 purpose test squarely covers, and the mechanism is specified in enough operational detail to exceed the "named without a programme" level (T1=2). Intelligent traffic management is simultaneously funded transport infrastructure (T5=2). Confidence is Moderate because the unit presents the system as what "can be made" rather than as a funded deployment; flagged on the mandatory T1 trigger.
53|0,0,0,0,2,0|directed|High||None|"Recently, the Ministry of Railways, Govt. of India has decided to use AI to undertake remote condition monitoring using non-intrusive sensors for monitoring signals, track circuits, axle counters and their sub-systems of interlocking, power supply systems including the voltage and current levels, relays, timers" -- a decided programme by a named ministry with specified scope (T5=2, directed). Every object monitored is rolling stock, signalling or power plant, which the Theme 1 negative boundary routes explicitly to Theme 5. Parking optimisation and vehicle-grid interaction are likewise infrastructure.
54|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A description of the global autonomous-vehicle research frontier: "most of the large investments in AI have been made in the field of autonomous vehicle", falling object-classification error rates, and the argument that "AI algorithms trained on Indian driving data have the potential to be very robust" (T5=1: market and technical context with no Indian instrument or commitment). The claim that Indian conditions produce globally valuable training data is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
55|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|An analysis of why sectoral barriers are common rather than sector-specific, using healthcare as the example: "while India has adopted electronic health record (EHR) policy, sharing of data between various hospital chains still remains a work in progress, since different hospital chains have adopted different interpretations of 'digitising records'" (T5=1: a diagnosis of interoperability failure with no instrument proposed here). The EHR policy is named without any of its content described, and the point made about it is technical rather than rights-based.
56|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|The unit closes the challenge analysis and opens the recommendations: "these recommendations have been formulated as fundamentally infrastructural in nature, and hence span across sectoral use cases", requiring "large scale transformational interventions, primarily led by the government, with private sector prov[iding]" support (T5=2: a programme of infrastructural interventions with a defined state-led delivery model, still at recommendation stage). "A desire to assume leadership in this nascent technology" is competitiveness framing (T6=1). At 351 words the unit is one word over the ceiling and is flagged.
57|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"Government of Karnataka's intention to set up a Centre of Excellence in AI in partnership with NASSCOM" is noted as an encouraging development, and "the first set of recommendations focus on turbocharging both core and applied research", with "two frameworks for solving some of AI's biggest research challenges through collaborative, market oriented approach" proposed alongside a workforce response to automation (T5=2: a defined research and skilling programme with named framework elements, at recommendation stage). No obligation, right, content or cross-border mechanism appears. At 341 words the unit sits inside the ceiling.
58|0,0,0,1,2,0|proposed|High||None|"Our recommendations to address these challenges include developing large foundational annotated data sets to democratise data and multistakeholder marketplaces across the AI value chain (data, annotated data and AI models)" (T5=2, proposed). "One of the key aspects of our ambition of #AIforAll includes responsible AI: ensuring adequate privacy, security and IP related concerns and balancing ethical considerations with need for innovation" states an ambition with no framework, body or obligation attached (T4=1). The unit is explicit that "the recommendations... are descriptive rather than prescriptive by design" and that specific funding targets have been "consciously avoided".
59|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A diagnosis of India's research position: "India produced a whopping 2.6 million STEM graduates in 2016, second only to China and more than 4 times the graduates produced by USA", yet "India sees itself lagging considerably in producing world-class research and innovation in most technology fields, more so in AI" (T5=1: capacity analysis with no instrument or programme committed here). The comparison with China and the USA is competitiveness framing (T6=1). At 362 words the unit exceeds the ceiling and is flagged.
60|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Further research diagnostics: "if we look at the country wise H-Index... India ranks a dismal 19th globally", and publication output is "heavily skewed in favour of top-15 institutes, that in total have contributed more than 42% of all research publications from 2001-2016" (T5=1: bibliometric analysis identifying a concentration problem, with no instrument proposed in this unit). The global ranking comparison is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
61|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|Moderate||None|"Looking at all the research publications from 2001-2016, only 14% of all publications have come from industry, with universities contributing 86%", and even that industry share "is dominated by Indian subsidiaries of international companies (~70%), with only one Indian company in top-10 (TCS)" (T5=1: a diagnosis of weak industrial research with no instrument). The dependence on foreign-owned subsidiaries is noted as a competitiveness weakness (T6=1). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is a short statistical fragment continuing the previous analysis.
62|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A description of the United States position: "The US Government is estimated to have spent USD1.2 billion in non-classified research in 2016-18 and the Defence Advanced Research Projects Agency (DARPA) is seeking a budget of USD3.44 billion in fiscal year 2019-20", with US firms dominating enterprise AI (T5=1: another country's instruments described, none adopted here). The account of US digital ecosystems and unicorn concentration is comparative competitiveness framing (T6=1).
63|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A description of China's approach: "a new development plan to create a USD150 billion domestic AI industry", "a national fund that supports research", government funding for AI schools at "the top 9 universities", and the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology "planning to put nearly USD950 million dollars per year into strategic AI projects for State Owned Enterprises and the public sector" (T5=1: a foreign state's industrial policy described, not adopted). "China has clear ambitions to be at the same level as the US by 2020 and the world leader in AI by 2030" is geostrategic framing (T6=1).
64|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A description of the United Kingdom's position: the value of "a plan with a governance structure and clear milestones" for inward investor confidence, Cambridge and Oxford as innovation centres, the DeepMind, VocalIQ and SwiftKey acquisitions, and Leverhulme Trust funding of "GBP80 million for research" (T5=1: another country's arrangements described). The framing is explicitly comparative -- what makes a country attractive to inward AI investment (T6=1).
65|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Continued international benchmarking: the Alan Turing Institute "announced as the national centre for AI in November 2017", the German Research Centre for Artificial Intelligence as "one of the world's largest AI research institutes", and "the EU's Robotics Public Private Partnership, launched in 2013... allocation of EUR700 million for research to 2020" with "an overall backing of EUR2.8 billion" (T5=1: foreign institutions and funding described, none adopted here). The survey of comparator institutions is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
66|1,0,0,0,2,0|enacted|Moderate||None|"The number of papers published has jumped 10 fold in last 10 years, from 331 papers in 2006 to 3,301 papers in 2016", and "IIT Bombay and IIT Patna have entered into a joint research collaboration with industry to focus on the applied aspects of AI" -- operating research activity with named institutions (T5=2, enacted). Within that collaboration's stated scope is "solving real-life social issues such as malnutrition, human trafficking and climate change through prediction and recommendation models using AI": trafficking prediction is a law-enforcement application whose object is people, named in a list with no programme, budget or implementing body, which is the T1=1 anchor. Confidence is Moderate because the application appears in a single clause.
67|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A statement of research constraints: "university research is largely restricted to theoretical or laboratory scale. This needs to be augmented with pilot projects / large scale test beds / laboratories", the difficulty of academic institutions constructing and operating test beds alone, and the absence of stakeholder connection to "convert outputs to outcomes" (T5=1: identified gaps with no instrument or funding proposed in this unit). No other theme is engaged.
68|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|The unit closes the constraint analysis -- "lack of large scale mission mode project management capabilities" -- and concludes that "incremental changes would not suffice and there is need for transformational changes to boost research major push coming from the government", opening the "Framework for promoting Artificial Intelligence Research in India" built on the Inter-Ministerial IM-ICPS Detailed Project Report (T5=2: a defined institutional framework with a named antecedent, at recommendation stage). Involving "technology translators" is a delivery mechanism rather than an obligation.
69|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"COREs will focus on core research of AI, and will take on the mantle of executing the responsibilities of both ICON and CROSS as per the IM-ICPS framework", while "ICTAIs will provide the ecosystem for application based technology development and deployment... an industry-led initiative" executing "CASTLE and CETIT" -- two proposed institution types with mandates mapped onto an existing inter-ministerial framework (T5=2, proposed). The unit is institutional design only; no rights, content, surveillance or cross-border mechanism appears.
70|0,0,0,1,2,1|proposed|High||None|"An umbrella organisation should be established to address issues relating to access to finance, social sustainability and the global competitiveness of the technologies developed. This body, which shall be recognised as the Centre for Studies on Technological Sustainability (CSTS)" is a named proposed institution with defined responsibilities (T5=2). One responsibility is "to monitor the impact of the AI technologies developed at the consumer level through social indices and recommend necessary modifications" -- impact monitoring with a recommendatory output and no audit obligation or remedy (T4=1). Modelling CSTS "on the lines of the Campus for Research Excellence and Technological Enterprise (CREATE), Singapore program or Innovate UK" and pursuing "global competitiveness" is international framing (T6=1).
71|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"To start with, COREs could be established at IISc, ISI and top IITs and IIITs", with multi-disciplinary linkages "with premier institutions in other disciplines viz. AIIMS for healthcare, TISS for arts and social science" and a "hub-and-spoke model" mentoring other institutes (T5=2: named host institutions and a defined delivery architecture, at recommendation stage). The proposed incentives -- "remunerations matching international standards" to "bring top-tier international faculty, especially the Indian diaspora" -- is talent competition with no control or exclusion mechanism (T6=1).
72|0,0,0,1,2,1|proposed|High||None|"The financial component of COREs for AI, which could be to the tune of INR 50 crore - INR 100 crore per CORE, should also include large scale funding for specific projects", allocated through "an application based model... wherein an applicant institute would have to demonstrate a viability plan" (T5=2: a quantified funding proposal with an allocation mechanism). "Explainable AI" appears as one of the listed CORE focus areas, a research subject rather than a transparency obligation owed to anyone (T4=1). The proposed "International Centers for Transformational Artificial Intelligence" carry an explicit international orientation (T6=1).
73|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"ICTAI Inc. should work proactively with private sector institutions to seek partnerships to set up the ICTAIs in priority domains", with "well-defined linkages with the COREs" and COREs "incentivised to commercialise their research at ICTAIs", plus a described selection process for ICTAI leadership and funding (T5=2: institutional design with defined governance and delivery mechanisms, at recommendation stage). The unit concerns research organisation only; no rights, content, surveillance or cross-border mechanism appears.
74|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"The ICTAIs should raise additional funding beyond this base funding, through philanthropic / private contributions, preferably in equity sharing mechanism", and "each ICTAI should have a Governance Board, comprising of industry leaders, academic luminaries and global thought leaders", vetted by ICTAI Inc. (T5=2: a proposed funding and governance architecture with defined roles). Seating "global thought leaders" on governance boards is international engagement without any agreement, funding cluster or control mechanism (T6=1).
75|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A description of a foreign institution: "The PRAIRIE Institute (PaRis Artificial Intelligence Research Institute) is a collaboration of industry and academia supported by the French government", with named public and corporate partners pursuing fundamental knowledge creation, applied impact and training (T5=1: another country's arrangement described, not adopted here). Its five-year objective "to bring together AI scientific and industrial leaders and make the PRAIRIE Institute a world leader in AI" and to become "an international benchmark" is comparative framing (T6=1).
76|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"For COREs and ICTAIs to discharge their duties effectively, pooled cloud infrastructure for AI applications should be made available. All the COREs and ICTAIs should be connected to the National Knowledge Network (NKN) and from there via a very high-speed link to a pooled cloud computing environment", with AIRAWAT proposed as "a cloud platform for Big Data Analytics and Assimilation, with a large, power-optimised AI Computing infrastructure" (T5=2: a specified national compute platform built on named existing network infrastructure). "Keeping national data secure" is a sovereignty rationale with no exclusion or control instrument attached (T6=1).
77|0,0,0,1,2,1|proposed|High||None|The AIRAWAT specification -- "multi-tenant multi-user computing support through resource partitioning", a "Deep Learning Software stack", "low latency High bandwidth network" and a "Mass Storage System to collect, store and share multi-petabytes of big data" -- is a detailed national compute infrastructure proposal (T5=2). Its listed utilisation includes "Anonymisation" alongside labelling and analytics, naming a privacy technique as a platform function without any obligation, standard or safeguard attached (T4=1). Benchmarking against "JAPAN's ABCI AI Supercomputing facility" is comparative framing (T6=1).
78|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"ICTAI Inc., the overall governing body for ICTAIs, could be the DARPA... equivalent of AI research in India. ICTAI Inc. should have the full autonomy on selecting the projects of importance and executing these projects, and should be trusted with a separate budget for pursuing such projects" (T5=2: a proposed institution with a dedicated budget and defined mandate). The DARPA analogy and the reference to Google's Duplex are comparative framing (T6=1). The unit debates whether industry or government should drive moonshot projects but creates no obligation on either.
79|0,1,0,0,2,0|proposed|Moderate||None|"Independence to select projects, design timelines and deliverables and allocate budgets shall fall within the ambit of the ICTAIs with no intervention of any government agency", together with a "fail fast model" giving "the autonomy to shut down projects and shift resources" (T5=2: a proposed research institution with defined operating rules). The explicit exclusion of any government agency from budget allocation and project selection proposes insulation from ordinary administrative and budgetary oversight, which registers weakly on Theme 2 as delegated autonomy stated without an instrument (T2=1). Confidence is Moderate because Theme 2's anchors concern executive override rather than agency independence.
80|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A case-study box on DARPA: created "in 1958 as response to increased US concerns that Soviet Union may have achieved technological superiority", with a founding mission "to prevent and create strategic surprise", and credited with the internet, GPS, stealth and drones (T5=1: a foreign institution described as a model, not adopted here). The Sputnik framing and the military origin of the agency are geostrategic context (T6=1). No Indian instrument, obligation or commitment appears.
81|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A case-study box on the CERN analogy, quoting Gary Marcus on "a huge, international collaboration, with thousands of scientists and billions of dollars of funding" that shares results "rather than restricting them to a single country or corporation", and recounting CERN's founding "in 1954 by 12 founding European nations" (T5=1: a description of a foreign institution as a model). The emphasis on "a truly international scientific collaboration" is international framing without any commitment in this unit (T6=1).
82|0,0,0,0,1,2|proposed|High||None|"To achieve #AIforAll... the Government of India should take the lead in bringing together the relevant parties to create People's AI, the CERN for AI" -- a proposal that India establish and lead a new multilateral research institution, supported by the precedent of India "leading the International Solar Alliance", a formalised international body (T6=2: a specified cross-border institutional proposal with a demonstrated model). The climate and solar commitments cited (100GW by 2022, 20GW installed) are context rather than AI programmes (T5=1). It remains a recommendation in a discussion paper.
83|0,0,0,1,1,2|proposed|High||None|The proposed focus areas for an international AI institute include "Opening the Black Box / Explainable AI", "Advanced anonymisation protocols for data security and privacy" and "Ethics in AI" -- research subjects rather than obligations, audits or rights (T4=1). "Declaration on Cooperation in Artificial Intelligence was signed by 25 states of European Union on 10th April 2018" and the call for "commitment and collaboration of truly international standards" cites a named multilateral instrument and proposes India join that class of cooperation (T6=2). OpenAI is cited as a private precedent (T5=1).
84|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Workforce diagnosis: "NASCCOM predicts that by 2022, a startling 46% of the Indian workforce will be engaged in entirely new jobs that do not exist today or jobs that have radically changed skill sets", demand for AI specialists rising 60%, and a projected shortfall of "2,00,000 data analytics professionals by 2020" (T5=1: labour-market projections with no programme, instrument or commitment in this unit). No other theme is engaged.
85|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Continued workforce analysis: the growth of India's middle class and millennial demographic as a market for technology products, and the observation that services jobs "will see a large overhaul" as automation spreads (T5=1). "As the permeation of automation increases, and protectionism among previous markets for these services becomes predominant" and the ambition "to establish itself as the future hub for AI related services" places the analysis in an international trade frame (T6=1).
86|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"As multinational companies move toward 'in-shoring' or 'no-shoring' due to development of automation technology and increasingly protectionist measures globally, it is of critical importance that the outsourcing industry (which currently accounts for roughly 15% of India's total labour force) adapts" (T5=1: a diagnosis of exposure with no instrument proposed here). The driver identified is foreign protectionism and the reshoring of outsourced work, which is an external trade condition rather than an Indian control mechanism (T6=1).
87|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"It is important to identify and promote creation of jobs that may replace traditional IT-BPM sector jobs in the future... Tasks such as data annotation, for example, have the potential to employ a large quantum of human resources", integrated with "relevant existing skilling initiatives" and new learning platforms (T5=2: a defined employment-generation strategy targeting a named job category, at recommendation stage). Serving "countries all over the world in otherwise capital intensive projects" is service-export framing (T6=1).
88|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|A case study of Samasource, whose "top objective as 'creating technology jobs for the impoverished while delivering the highest quality work in a cost-effective manner'" is delivered through community identification, assessment and "a 3-week dedicated machine learning and image annotation training track" (T5=1: a foreign private-sector model described as illustrative, with no Indian commitment). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is an external case study rather than policy text.
89|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"Implementation of recognised certificate courses through higher education institutions could be a major boost to recognising resources spent on re-skilling, and holding these institutions to standards in delivery of knowledge" -- a proposed recognition and standardisation mechanism for informal training providers, responding to the finding that "their standard of education is hard to assess for companies looking to hire" (T5=2). The INSOFE example, "certification recognised by the Language Technologies Institute of Carnegie Mellon University", is international accreditation framing (T6=1).
90|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High|YES|Conflicting Statutory Mandates|"It is thus suggested that co-funded models between the government and companies be explored", with incentives that "could include payroll taxes which are dedicated to subsidising training opportunities, income tax deductions for companies participating in reskilling initiatives, special taxes to be paid if a minimum training budget is not disbursed, as well as public grants" (T5=2). The special-tax option places an addressee -- employers -- under a fiscal consequence for failing to meet a minimum training budget, which would be a coercive industrial mandate; but it is offered as one item in a list of options to be "explored", with no rate, threshold or enacting vehicle specified, so the instrument condition of the Score-3 Evidence Test fails. Two of three conditions are met, Theme 5 is capped at 2, and the unit is flagged per codebook 2.2.
91|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"In primary and secondary schools, there is a need for transition to skill based education in subjects relevant to AI", with "increased amount of project related work across education levels, promoting schemes like the establishment of ATLs (Atal Tinkering Labs) in schools, necessary change in curricula" (T5=2: a named existing scheme plus specified curricular measures, at recommendation stage). The unit concerns education capacity only; no rights, content, surveillance or cross-border mechanism appears.
92|0,0,0,1,2,0|proposed|High||None|"Bridge courses in AI for post-graduates in non-computer science or data science domains", one-year cross-domain courses, and "an institutional framework in place that is responsive to changing scenarios" are proposed education measures (T5=2). "A standing committee or task force comprising of all stakeholders may be constituted by the government with the objective of examining and reporting the changes in employment caused by AI in India" proposes a standing body to monitor labour-market impact, with a reporting rather than an enforcement mandate (T4=1).
93|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Global adoption context: "a 2017 survey by Statista finds that 78% of firms globally are either using AI extensively, or have plans for use in near future", while McKinsey finds "deployment at scale has been rare" with "only 20% us[ing] any AI relat[ed]" technology at scale (T5=1: survey findings with no Indian instrument or commitment). "Firms in China and the US especially, are proactively engaging with their research communities to enable early adoption and position themselves competitively" is comparative framing (T6=1).
94|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"India's IT industry with over USD160 billion annual revenue, has yet to build pioneering AI / ML capabilities commensurate with its potential", though "the top 5 IT service companies have begun to use AI", with Wipro's Holmes, TCS's Ignio and Infosys's Nia named (T5=1: industry description with the conclusion that intervention is needed, but no instrument specified here). "Lest India lose the chance to secure a prominent position on the global AI map" is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
95|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Barriers to adoption: industry-specific data "concentrated in the hands of a few major players", making it "difficult for new entrants to deliver tailor made services that can compete with data rich incumbents such as Facebook or Google", and "high cost and low availability of computing infrastructure" (T5=1: a diagnosis of market failure with no instrument in this unit). "Lack of infrastructure has led to many Indian AI startups to incorporate their business outside the country" identifies a cross-border capital and capability leakage (T6=1).
96|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|An analysis of why the AI value chain raises entry barriers: small firms cannot "vertically integrate all these processes", and without "a strong, stable price discovery mechanism" incentives across the value chain will not align, compounded by "the difficulty of acquiring the raw material (data) in the first place" (T5=1: a market-structure diagnosis motivating the marketplace recommendation that follows). No obligation, right, content or cross-border mechanism appears.
97|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|The marketplace recommendation is developed with defined effects: "encouraging specialisation in different niches by firms and creation of novel business models", so that "a new segment of startups could entirely focus on the problem of curating raw data to be put on the marketplace", and "unlocking new sources of data and enabling more efficient use of computational and human resources" (T5=2: a specified market mechanism with an articulated theory of how it changes firm behaviour, at recommendation stage).
98|0,0,0,2,2,0|proposed|High|YES|Conflicting Statutory Mandates|"There is no mechanism currently to ensure that appropriate permissions are taken from the actual data owners before data custodians share the data", and under the proposed formal marketplace "it would become necessary for firms to take consent of individuals getting imaged to aggregate and sell their data. This could create mechanisms through which individuals are ultimately compensated" (T4=2). An addressee (data custodians and firms) and an instrument (the proposed formal marketplace with consent conditions) are present, but no penalty, audit or exclusion attaches to non-compliance, so two of the three Score-3 conditions are met, Theme 4 is capped at 2, and the unit is flagged per codebook 2.2. Recommendation 2 on "facilitating creation of large foundational annotated data sets" supplies the economic mechanism (T5=2).
99|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"It is necessary for the government to explore assistance in building of large corpora of data across domains, as a means of laying the foundation for startups and enterprises to build applications and services which are tailor-made to the Indian context, and in the process lowering entry barr[iers]" (T5=2: a defined state role in building foundational data assets). The unit also documents the annotation industry -- "Alegion, Scale, CloudFactory, Mighty AI, and CrowdFlower... have received about USD50 million in investment funding" -- as market evidence.
100|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"In India, Academic Entrepreneurship is being taken up through mechanisms such as the development of dedicated e-cells in institutions such as IIT Bombay, IIT Madras and IIM Ahmedabad", and "collaboration between Research Organisations is required to promote the 'AI+X' paradigm" (T5=2: existing institutional mechanisms plus a specified collaboration model). "Several successful US universities have co-located departments which enabled close collaboration" is comparative framing, and MNC knowledge transfer is noted as an existing partnership channel (T6=1).
101|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|"Collaboration with trade bodies and Venture Capitals is essential for successful functioning of a profitable business", with such bodies helping firms share problems and solutions and access ground-level learning (T5=2: a defined collaboration mechanism within the adoption recommendations). Trade bodies are also credited with "collectively identifying new international markets for such products / technologies and in lobbying/negotiating for favourable trade norms with the national and international bodies" -- market access and norm advocacy conducted by industry associations rather than by the state (T6=1). At 350 words the unit is exactly at the ceiling.
102|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|Recommendation 5 proposes that "incubation hubs specifically for AI startups in collaboration with State Governments and private sector stakeholders need to be set up to provide space, and other infrastructure facilities", and the "establishment of fund to provide grant funding to startups to facilitate their operation and business" (T5=2: named delivery vehicles with defined instruments). The unit also records existing capacity building "with organisations such as Google to organise capacity building training and workshops for government functionaries".
103|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|The data marketplace case: incumbents "enjoy an oligopoly" because they can "buy data in the informal market setting" and run "specialised departments", while "in the process of informal data acquisition as well, there is no proper price discovery mechanism in place" and "data providers are likely to be substantially underpaid" (T5=2: the formal marketplace is proposed as the corrective mechanism, with a specified market failure it is designed to address). No obligation or penalty attaches to any party.
104|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|An analysis of the properties of data -- permanence, ubiquity, the transport complexity of growing datasets -- and the resulting difficulties: "the primary assumption of a data marketplace in operation is that data custodians will share the data on the marketplace. Organisations may be reluctant to do so", and transaction fees and rigid pricing "add to inefficiencies" (T5=1: a critique of the proposed mechanism's feasibility, with no instrument added here).
105|0,0,0,2,2,0|proposed|High||None|"The process of exchange, with enforced provisions of privacy and anonymisation, brings a market determined value to data and thus forces the existing informal data exchange economy, without any privacy protection, to move towards a formal economy", and "the government can establish a committee of experts, researchers, AI developers and regulators to create the standards the data marketplace will adhere by" -- proposed institutional privacy governance with a named standard-setting body including regulators, which is the T4=2 anchor. The exchange itself is the economic mechanism (T5=2). The Ocean Protocol blockchain curation model is cited as a design precedent.
106|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|Design features for the annotation marketplace: "verification mechanisms such as multiple annotation, verification of samples by trained professionals", and "establishing verified traceability... to identify poor annotators, and create mechanisms to reduce the effect of error generated", benchmarked against MTurk, Figure-Eight and ClickWorker (T5=2: specified quality-assurance mechanisms for a proposed market). Identifying poor annotators is quality control within a commercial platform, not state monitoring of persons, so Theme 1 is not engaged.
107|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"A crowdsourced platform for annotation, on the lines of the highly popular government's citizen participation platform myGov.in, can be established by the government (to be operated by a private player) where seed data for annotation can be provided by the organisations mentioned above", using existing government datasets that are "largely unused due to lack of proper annotation, prohibitive cost associated with the process, and lack of proper mechanisms for acquisition" (T5=2: a proposed state-established platform with a named operating model and data sources).
108|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|The deployable model segment "should focus on defined deployable models that can be bought or sold... either be bought and sold as data, or more likely, as a service or an API layer that can be charged per use", attracting enterprises that "post problems which they are seeking to be solved using AI tools", with "governments... another party to announce specific problems which they intend to be solved" (T5=2: a specified market design with defined participants and transaction forms). No obligation, right, content or cross-border mechanism appears.
109|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|Implementation design for the marketplace: "the platform should be able to prescribe some basic standards for data sharing which will allow non-ML savvy PSUs to post problems and data", and "incentives to attract both seekers and providers initially will need to be figured out. PSUs and government agencies may act as initial seeders/user[s]" (T5=2: a specified platform design with a state-led adoption strategy). The standards described are technical data-sharing specifications rather than privacy obligations.
110|2,0,1,0,2,0|proposed|Moderate|YES|Polysemic Uncertainty|The demand-side use case list for the National AI Marketplace opens with "traffic authorities and police: real-time object detection in CCTV video feeds, face detection and object tracking, number plate detection" -- facial recognition, person tracking and number-plate identification specified as procurement categories for police, whose object is unambiguously people and whose purpose is enforcement. The mechanism is named with operational specificity and attributed to a named class of state user, which takes it past the "named without a programme" level to T1=2, though no budget or deployment is attached. "All India Radio and Prasar Bharti: speech-to-text services for transcription and analysis" and document sentiment analysis place state broadcast and document content under automated analysis without any obligation on any party (T3=1). Agriculture, freight and citizen chatbots supply the economic baseline (T5=2). Confidence is Moderate because the list is a procurement demand signal rather than a funded programme; flagged on the mandatory T1 trigger.
111|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|"Technology adoption, especially AI, can significantly improve operational efficiency and drive down operational costs incurred by PSUs operating in financially unsustainable but socially critical areas", and "the NAIM allows the public sector firms to directly scout for solutions which they can use in their business operations" (T5=2: a specified role for the proposed marketplace in public-sector modernisation). The constraints described -- regulations on "procurement, manpower hiring" -- are noted as context rather than targeted for exemption.
112|0,0,0,2,2,0|proposed|High||None|"Instead of attempting to build such marketplaces, which it is not equipped to do so, the government should come out with enabling regulations... These would include regulations and standard permissions for any personal data being sold, standards for anonymisation, standards for ensuring annotation accuracy, and cybersecurity standards for the module dealing with deployable models" -- a proposed regulatory framework governing the sale of personal data with named components, which is the T4=2 anchor for a published framework short of enumerated obligations and penalties. "It is proposed that a decentralised data marketplace based on distributed ledger technology be set up" and the government may "build the NAIM platform where all relevant stakeholders can sign up" (T5=2).
113|0,0,0,1,1,0|proposed|High||None|The chapter opening frames the problem: "any strategy document on promoting AI, necessarily needs to be conscious of the probable factors of the AI ecosystem that may undermine ethical conduct, impinge on one's privacy and undermine the security protocol", distinguishing ethics (bias propagation), privacy ("collection and inappropriate use of data for personal discrimination") and security (accountability) (T4=1: an issue statement with no framework, body or obligation proposed in this unit). At 356 words the unit exceeds the ceiling and is flagged.
114|0,0,0,1,1,0|proposed|High||None|"One possible way to approach this would be to identify the in-built biases and assess their impact, and in turn find ways to reduce the bias. This reactive approach, use-case based, may help till the time we find techniques to bring neutrality to data feeding AI solutions", followed by the diagnosis of the "Black Box Phenomenon" (T4=1: a proposed analytical approach to fairness with no instrument, oversight body or obligation attached). The unit calls for "a combined dialogue and sustained research" rather than a mechanism.
115|0,0,0,1,1,0|proposed|High||None|"Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) is an evolving area of research", illustrated by DARPA's XAI programme aiming to "produce more explainable models, while maintaining a high level of learning performance" and "enable human users to understand, appropriately trust, and effectively manage" AI systems, with the normative claim that "the machine learning algorithms of tomorrow should have the built-in capability to explain their logic" (T4=1: explainability as a research direction and a design aspiration, not an obligation owed to anyone). The research programme described is foreign (T5=1).
116|0,0,0,1,1,0|proposed|High||None|"Data collection without proper consent, privacy of personal data, inherent selection biases and resultant risk of profiling and discrimination, and non-transparent nature of AI solutions are some of the issues requiring deliberation and proper recourse" (T4=1: an enumeration of privacy harms with recourse left undefined). The profiling described is by companies "harvesting significant amounts of consumer data and using it inappropriately", which is commercial rather than state monitoring, so Theme 1 is not engaged.
117|0,0,0,3,0,1|proposed|Moderate||None|"The work being done by Justice Srikrishna Committee on data protection law is very opportune and timely. The 7-core principles of data protection and privacy - informed consent, technology agnosticism, data controller accountability, data minimisation, holistic application, deterrent penalties and structured enforcement - are quite comprehensive and should provide a strong privacy protection regime in the country once enacted." Instrument (an enumerated framework whose obligations are identifiable), addressee (data controllers) and consequence ("deterrent penalties and structured enforcement") are all present, so T4=3 under the permissive reading of Evidence Test condition 1; binding_status is proposed because the regime is not yet law. Confidence is Moderate because the strict-versus-permissive reading of condition 1 remains an open PI item. Japanese and German sectoral frameworks are cited as models (T6=1).
118|0,0,0,2,0,1|proposed|High||None|"European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) guidelines, which have been enforced in May 2018, encourage design of less-privacy invasive systems. French laws give a right to explanation for administrative algorithmic decisions, making it much more comprehensive than GDPR on administrative decisions" -- foreign instruments described with some of their content, against which "India's privacy protection regime will have to be continually updated" (T4=2: a stated direction of regulatory development, with the enforceable instruments belonging to other jurisdictions). "Encourage AI developers to adhere to international standards", citing the IEEE Global Initiative's chapter on "Personal Data and Individual Access Control in Ethically Aligned Design", is voluntary international alignment (T6=1).
119|0,0,0,2,1,1|proposed|High|YES|Missing Operational Context|"India should collaborate on areas of research like Differential Privacy, Privacy by Design, Safety-Critical AI and Multi-Party Computations which enable protection of privacy despite data sharing at a wide scale" is a funded-research direction for privacy technology (T4=2, alongside the awareness commitment). "Privacy has been termed as a fundamental right by the Supreme Court of India" cites a binding constitutional holding but describes none of its content or consequences, so under codebook 8.2 the unit is flagged for missing operational context and Theme 4 is not lifted by an instrument the text does not describe. The research collaboration is both economic capacity (T5=1) and international engagement (T6=1). At 345 words the unit sits inside the ceiling.
120|0,0,0,2,1,0|proposed|High|YES|Conflicting Statutory Mandates|"One possible framework that can be mooted involves the following components: a. Negligence test for damages caused by AI software, as opposed to strict liability. This involves self-regulation by the stakeholders by conducting damage impact assessment at every stage of development of an AI model. b. As an extension of the negligence test, safe harbours need to be formulated to insulate or limit liability". An addressee (AI developers) and a consequence (civil liability under a negligence standard, with impact assessment at every development stage) are both present, but the instrument is explicitly "mooted" with no legislative vehicle, so two of the three Score-3 conditions are met, Theme 4 is capped at 2, and the unit is flagged. Note that the proposed direction limits rather than expands liability. At 341 words the unit sits inside the ceiling.
121|0,0,0,0,2,0|proposed|High||None|The summary table of government actions opens with research: "identify academic institutions, provide fiscal support to establish COREs focusing on core technology research in AI" and "institute National AI Fellowships to retain outgoing PhD students and attract researchers", with the role of government defined as "a facilitator, an active promoter and wherever required, of an owner" (T5=2: named instruments with a defined state role). At 424 words the unit exceeds the ceiling and is flagged; it is a table rendered as prose, which is why it runs long.
122|0,0,0,1,2,0|proposed|High||None|Table rows covering skills: "set up AI / Data Science training standards, as per National Skills Qualification Framework, and provide certifications to training institutes", "incentivise investment in training of employees through tax breaks and grants for employers", credit-bearing MOOCs, bridge courses, and "introduce AI modules in Atal Tinkering Labs" (T5=2: named instruments mapped to an existing national qualification framework). "Constitute a standing committee or taskforce to examine and report on changes in employment induced by adoption AI" is a monitoring body with a reporting mandate (T4=1).
123|0,0,0,1,2,0|proposed|High||None|Table rows covering data: "explore partnerships and co-fund building of large corpora of data across domains", "announce grand challenge tasks for tagging of images, text or videos, and devising reward based mechanisms", and co-funding "the establishment of: 1. Data marketplace 2. Data annotation marketplace 3. Deployable model marketplace" (T5=2: named instruments with a defined state financing role). "Create governance guidelines" for the National AI Marketplace names a governance instrument without specifying its obligations (T4=1).
124|0,0,0,2,2,0|proposed|High||None|"Set up a consortium of Ethics Councils at each CORE and ICTAI to define the standard practices and monitor their adoption", "address and implement data protection legal framework, which protects human rights and privacy without stifling innovation in India", and "collaborate with industry to come out with sector specific guidelines on privacy, security and ethics - on manufacturing, financial services, identity, telecommunication, robotics" -- named governance bodies with a monitoring function plus a proposed statutory framework and sectoral guidelines, which is the T4=2 anchor. "Establish incubation hubs and venture funds specifically for AI startups in collaboration with State Governments" is the economic instrument (T5=2).
125|0,0,0,0,0,0|n/a|High||None|Appendix material explaining machine learning: "machine learning algorithms detect patterns and learn how to make predictions and recommendations by processing data and experiences, rather than by receiving explicit programming instruction", followed by a comparison table of supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning. The unit is a technical definition with no policy content, no actor under obligation, no programme and no normative claim, so all six themes are 0 and binding_status is n/a.
126|0,0,0,0,0,0|n/a|High||None|A continuation of the appendix comparison table, setting out when to use each type of machine learning and how each works, using housing prices, customer journeys and portfolio trading as illustrations. It is instructional material about algorithm classes with no policy content of any kind, so all six themes are 0 and binding_status is n/a.
127|0,0,0,0,0,0|n/a|High||None|Appendix material defining deep learning: "interconnected layers of software-based calculators known as 'neurons' form a neural network. The network can ingest vast amounts of input data and process them through multiple layers that learn increasingly complex featu[res]". The unit is a technical definition with no policy content, no actor under obligation and no normative claim, so all six themes are 0 and binding_status is n/a.
128|0,0,0,0,0,0|n/a|High||None|A worked mechanical illustration of convolutional and recurrent neural networks -- how a CNN classifies the letter "A" from pixels and how an RNN carries state across a sentence. It is instructional appendix material with no policy content of any kind, so all six themes are 0 and binding_status is n/a.
129|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|The unit closes the RNN illustration and opens Appendix II's global review with the observation that "countries around the world are becoming increasingly aware of the potential economic and social benefits of developing and applying AI", citing that "China and U.K. estimate that 26% and 10% of their GDPs respectively in 2030 will be sourced from AI-related activi[ties]" (T5=1: economic projections attributed to other states). The comparative GDP framing is competitiveness context (T6=1); no Indian instrument is proposed.
130|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A country-by-country summary of thematic priorities: China on public service delivery "through financial support, developing talent pipeline, and leveraging international cooperation", Japan "moving from the 'Industry 4.0' paradigm to 'Society 5.0'", and France's areas of interest (T5=1: foreign programmes described, none adopted here). The survey format is comparative benchmarking (T6=1).
131|0,1,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"France is trying to streamline its innovation track with 'innovation sandboxes' which would provide an open platform for innovation and offer resources for use in field-testing" -- a foreign regulatory sandbox described but not adopted by India, so Theme 2 registers only at the level of a mechanism referenced without commitment (T2=1). "China is focused on developing open source innovation platforms in partnership with private players like Baidu, Alibaba, and Tencent", with funding "for 5G networks... and deployment of supercomputers" (T5=1). The comparative survey is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
132|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Japan has convened a 'national consultative body' with 3 universities and the Japan business foundation to develop education programs for reskilling", and "China has launched a five-year university program to train at least 500 teachers and 5,000 students", with a plan for "50 world-class teaching institutes... 50 national level high quality online open courses and 50 AI faculties by 2020 as part of the 'AI+X' program" (T5=1: foreign skills programmes described, none adopted). The benchmarking purpose is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
133|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|A comparison of research capacity at leading foreign universities: faculty and student counts, an undergraduate AI degree, IBM's Watson developed "in research collaboration with CMU", the MIT Intelligence initiative, and the AI4ALL programme "to increase diversity in the field of AI research" (T5=1: foreign institutional capacity described). The unit is a benchmarking table rendered as prose (T6=1).
134|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Alibaba's Aliyun is working with the National Engineering laboratory on big data systems and software at the Tsinghua University", while in Japan "Riken Centre for Advanced Intelligence Project and Advanced Industrial Science and Technology are the nodal research institutes for industry collaboration" (T5=1: foreign research-industry arrangements described, none adopted here). The account of how other countries organise industry-academia collaboration is comparative framing (T6=1).
135|0,1,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Most of the governments have established / utilised existing centralised umbrella body for budgetary planning of AI interventions and for formulating strategy and drafting policies", naming "the National Science and Technology Council in the USA, Strategic Council for AI technologies in Japan, AI council in the U.K." and noting that "the UAE has a Minister of State for AI" (T5=1). The pattern described is centralisation of AI policy authority in a single executive body -- a Theme 2 mechanism, but observed in other countries and not adopted here (T2=1). Comparative governance benchmarking (T6=1).
136|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Foreign funding commitments catalogued: the UK's "GBP725 million industrial strategy challenge fund", "GBP1.7 billion for transforming cities fund", a "GBP2.5 billion investment fund at the British Business Bank" and "reforming rules for pension funds to mandate inclusion of AI in their investment portfolio", plus Japan's plan "to increase its science and innovation budget by JPY900 billion by 2020" (T5=1: other states' instruments described, including a mandatory portfolio rule that India does not adopt). Comparative benchmarking (T6=1).
137|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|An investment table rendered as prose: AI investment by tech giants and other corporations in 2016 split across M&A, internal corporate investment, private equity and venture capital, with compound annual growth rates (T5=1: market data with no policy content). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is a table whose column structure does not survive extraction cleanly, which limits how much can be read from it.
138|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|Moderate||None|A product inventory of the technology giants: virtual assistants (Google Now, Alexa, DuerOS), AlphaGo, self-driving cars, Rekognition image analysis, Tmall Genie, and a "smart city AI platform developed for Kuala Lumpur" (T5=1: market description). The Kuala Lumpur deployment records a Chinese smart-city platform exported to another jurisdiction, which is cross-border technology diffusion described rather than any Indian mechanism (T6=1). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is a badly-extracted comparison table.
139|0,0,0,1,1,1|proposed|High||None|"China has formed a 'national team' with large private players including Baidu and Tencent to undertake fundamental and applied research", with "Baidu... working with the Chinese government to develop brain-inspired intelligent technology" (T5=1, T6=1: a foreign state-industry arrangement described). "EU has released a comprehensive legal framework for data protection called General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). This framework details the rights of individuals (consent, data portability, etc.), obl[igations]" describes a foreign enacted instrument with some of its content, but India adopts nothing here, so Theme 4 registers only as a reference (T4=1).
140|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|"Governments are setting themselves up for success with support from the private sector and academia. However, the models of engagement vary depending on starting points, challenges and appetite for public funding and regulation", closing with the warning that countries that "decide to wait for a few years" will not "match up to the current momentum" (T5=1: a comparative conclusion with no instrument). The urgency argument is competitiveness framing (T6=1); this text repeats material from the main body.
141|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|Moderate||None|A country funding table: Belgium's FWO and FNRS agencies with "approximately EUR1.8 million per year" and "approximately EUR6.7 million per year" respectively and their AI application success rates, and China's governments "giving USD800,000 to USD1 million in subsidies to A[I startups]" (T5=1: foreign funding instruments catalogued, none adopted). Comparative benchmarking (T6=1). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is a table rendered as prose.
142|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|Moderate||None|Continued funding table: "the Irish government spends, through the Irish Economic Development Agency (IDA), Enterprise Ireland, and Science Foundation Ireland, over EUR700 million on R&D annually", and "the Israeli government has several grant funding schemes for promoting collaboration and knowledge transfer between academia and industry, such as Magnet and Magneton" (T5=1: foreign instruments catalogued). Comparative benchmarking (T6=1). Confidence is Moderate for the same table-extraction reason.
143|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|Moderate||None|Further funding entries: UK research programmes on machine learning and "Natural Artificial Intelligence", Spain's "EUR170 million... invested in the Industry 4.0 project Industria Conectada 4.0 under the National R&D and Innovation Plan", and Sweden's "Research Institutes of Sweden... setting up an AI centre (RISE AI)" (T5=1: foreign instruments catalogued, none adopted). Comparative benchmarking (T6=1). Confidence is Moderate because the unit is table content.
144|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|Appendix III opens by locating the binding constraint: "the leading algorithms are available as software packages either commercially or open source", storage and compute "has become commoditised", and "the challenge still remains with data - access to high quality, reliable data along with appropriate mark-ups still remains a challenge" (T5=1: a factor-of-production analysis with no instrument proposed in this unit). No other theme is engaged.
145|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|High||None|A case study of ImageNet: "a large visual database designed for use in visual object recognition software research", where "over 14 million URLs of images have been hand annotated by ImageNet to indicate what objects are pictured" (T5=1: an illustrative precedent for the foundational-dataset recommendation, with no Indian commitment in this unit). The database annotates objects in images rather than identifying persons.
146|2,0,0,2,2,0|proposed|Moderate|YES|Polysemic Uncertainty|"Corporates based in India may be mandated to share their data for social good. For example, sharing transportation pattern of individuals / mass transits, collected by service providers and aggregators, can help the city planners help in planning routes, predicting and managing traffic." A compulsory transfer of individual movement traces from private aggregators to the state engages Theme 1 directly: the Theme 1 boundary clarification holds that movement analytics remains Theme 1 even under an urban-planning framing (T1=2). The same proposal is a data-sharing mandate on a class of firms, but the instrument is unspecified -- "may be mandated" with no vehicle, threshold or penalty -- so two of the three Score-3 conditions are met and Theme 5 is capped at 2 and flagged. "Upon proper and informed consent from the citizens, these anonymised data may be shared" proposes consent and anonymisation as the safeguard (T4=2). Confidence is Moderate because the mandate is raised as a possibility rather than designed.
147|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|An inventory table of public benchmark datasets -- MNIST, CIFAR, ImageNet, Labeled Faces in the Wild, TREC, WikiText and others -- with sizes, publishers and countries of origin (T5=1: a research-infrastructure inventory supporting the foundational-dataset argument). "Labeled Faces In The Wild" is listed as an academic benchmark corpus, not as a deployed identification system, so Theme 1 is not engaged. Confidence is Moderate because the unit is table content.
148|0,0,0,0,2,1|proposed|High||None|The dataset inventory closes with a directional conclusion: "it is clearly evident that universities and research laboratories in the USA in particular, have been the leaders in sharing these datasets, which has contributed significantly in developing a strong AI research base in the USA. The Government of India can also play a crucial role, working with the various academic institutions, in making annotated India specific data available for advancing AI research" -- a defined state role in building national research data assets (T5=2). The comparison with US dataset leadership is competitiveness framing (T6=1).
149|0,0,0,0,1,1|proposed|High||None|Appendix IV argues that "sectors that are most pursued by VCs could be a good proxy for sectors to be focused on", citing that "AI remains the most active industry vertical in VC funding landscape, with an aggregate total investment of more than USD31 billion globally across more than 3,600 disclosed deals in past 5 years" (T5=1: an investment-based method for sector selection, with no instrument). The global funding landscape is comparative context (T6=1).
150|0,0,0,0,1,0|proposed|Moderate||None|A description of one company's healthcare AI portfolio: Google's focus on "eye diseases (diabetic retinopathy), diabetes (detection and management), heart disease... Parkinson's disease and multiple sclerosis", noting that its "AI algorithm for diabetic retinopathy, trained on 128,000 images, was on-par with a panel of ophthalmologists" (T5=1: market evidence for sector prioritisation). The systems described diagnose the patients they examine, so the Theme 1 purpose test is not met. Confidence is Moderate because the unit is the truncated tail of an appendix.
